                "senderName": "NWS Salt Lake City UT",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued October 12 at 1:34PM MDT until October 12 at 6:00PM MDT by NWS Salt Lake City UT",
                "description": "* WHAT...Flash flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...A portion of Utah, including the following areas, Castle\nCountry, Central Mountains, Eastern Juab/Millard Counties, San\nRafael Swell, Sanpete Valley, Sevier Valley, Wasatch Plateau/Book\nCliffs, Bryce Canyon Country, Capitol Reef National Park and\nVicinity, Glen Canyon Recreation Area/Lake Powell, South Central\nUtah, Southern Mountains, Upper Sevier River Valleys, Western\nCanyonlands, Lower Washington County, Southwest Utah and Zion\nNational Park. This also includes recent burn scars such as\nForsyth, Cottonwood, Silver King, France Canyon, and Monroe Canyon.\n\n* WHEN...Until 6 PM MDT this evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, dry washes, slot canyons, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations such as slickrock areas and recent burn\nscars. Creeks and streams may rise out of their banks. Flooding may\noccur in poor drainage and urban areas.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Anomalously high moisture will continue to impact the region.\nWhile this is expected to be a long duration rainfall event,\nscattered instances of heavier rainfall rates are expected with\nstronger storms yielding flash flooding concerns given saturated\nsoils and prior burn scars.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "You should monitor later forecasts and be prepared to take action\nshould Flash Flood Warnings be issued.",
                "response": "Prepare",
                "note": null,
